Capstone:

2
00:00:01.547 --> 00:00:03.766
The Grocery Marketplace, End to End.

3
00:00:04.046 --> 00:00:08.163
Chapter 56, from the Capstone part of the System Design Playbook.

4
00:00:09.913 --> 00:00:10.873
At a glance:

5
00:00:11.153 --> 00:00:12.327
Chapter at a glance.

6
00:00:12.607 --> 00:00:13.609
Reader bands:

7
00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:29.377
B3, then B4, Prerequisites: the whole handbook, especially Ch 00 (the platform and its 26 services), Ch 18, Ch 27, Ch 31 to 32 and case studies 38 to 55.

8
00:00:29.657 --> 00:00:32.580
What you will build: the whole platform as one design.

9
00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:51.057
It covers requirements and capacity; the service and data architecture; the event backbone; the critical paths with their latency budgets; failure isolation; SLOs that are arithmetically achievable; deployment topology; security, compliance and cost; and an evolution roadmap.

10
00:00:51.337 --> 00:00:53.620
Every earlier chapter solved one part.

11
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.225
This chapter checks that the parts compose.

12
00:00:56.505 --> 00:00:57.572
The four labs:

13
00:00:57.852 --> 00:01:01.031
L1: latency budget of place-order (simulation).

14
00:01:01.311 --> 00:01:02.527
1M checkouts.

15
00:01:03.877 --> 00:01:10.447
The external payment gateway is most of the median (~180 of 222 ms).

16
00:01:10.727 --> 00:01:18.727
Our own part's p99 is ~1.2 s, even though each hop's own p99 is under 60 ms:

17
00:01:19.007 --> 00:01:22.911
0.5% outliers across 7 hops compound.

18
00:01:23.191 --> 00:01:36.653
Timeouts plus honest degradation for optional calls, and hedged pricing, cut our p99 from 1,231 to 1,050 ms, degrading 1.06% of checkouts.

19
00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:42.714
A page that needs 30 calls waits on a p99-slow one 26% of the time.

20
00:01:42.994 --> 00:01:48.135
L2: the whole checkout under failure (real HTTP + PostgreSQL).

21
00:01:48.415 --> 00:01:52.767
2,000 checkouts, with lost payment replies and orchestrator crashes.

22
00:01:54.117 --> 00:02:03.013
The naive design produced 60 customers charged with no order, 139 double charges and 44 leaked reservations.

23
00:02:03.293 --> 00:02:08.392
Idempotency, a payment-status check and a saga log removed the double charges.

24
00:02:08.672 --> 00:02:11.893
Adding a recovery sweeper brought every invariant to zero.

25
00:02:12.173 --> 00:02:18.019
L3: blast radius of each service (real graph analysis of the 26-service map).

26
00:02:18.299 --> 00:02:19.152
As-built:

27
00:02:19.432 --> 00:02:23.784
22 services could break user traffic, and 16 could break checkout.

28
00:02:24.064 --> 00:02:27.520
Identity alone took down 100% of requests.

29
00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:43.099
Designed: local token checks, async side effects and soft optional features left 15 and 10, and cut total traffic lost across all single failures from 649% to 385%.

30
00:02:43.379 --> 00:02:44.254
L4:

31
00:02:44.534 --> 00:02:46.795
SLO composition (simulation).

32
00:02:47.075 --> 00:02:54.393
Ten hard dependencies at 99.95% each give a checkout of 99.46%.

33
00:02:54.673 --> 00:02:58.107
Every month broke a 99.95% budget.

34
00:02:58.387 --> 00:03:00.158
Shared databases make it worse.

35
00:03:00.438 --> 00:03:03.553
Cells cap one outage at a quarter to a half of customers.

36
00:03:04.903 --> 00:03:11.367
Even 99.995% per service reached only 99.94%.

37
00:03:11.647 --> 00:03:15.060
Journey SLOs must be set with this arithmetic in hand.

38
00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:16.321
Reading time:

39
00:03:16.601 --> 00:03:18.521
120 min + code.

40
00:03:19.871 --> 00:03:20.725
Part 1.

41
00:03:21.005 --> 00:03:22.925
Introduction and Why It Matters.

42
00:03:24.175 --> 00:03:26.329
A story: every service was green.

43
00:03:26.609 --> 00:03:27.591
Sale night.

44
00:03:27.871 --> 00:03:37.492
Every service dashboard was green: each service met its 99.95% SLO, and each team's p99 was under 60 ms.

45
00:03:37.772 --> 00:03:38.433
But:

46
00:03:38.713 --> 00:03:41.487
The checkout success rate was 91%.

47
00:03:41.767 --> 00:03:44.817
Support queues were full of money deducted, no order.

48
00:03:45.097 --> 00:03:47.124
Some customers were charged twice.

49
00:03:47.404 --> 00:03:56.577
For 12 minutes, nobody could log in, because the identity service hit its connection limit and every request validated tokens there, not just logins.

50
00:03:57.927 --> 00:04:03.005
The home page stalled when the recommendation service slowed, because the page waited for it.

51
00:04:03.285 --> 00:04:07.487
In the post-mortem, the service owners were right that their services were fine.

52
00:04:07.767 --> 00:04:09.090
The platform was not.

53
00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:21.018
Its guarantees are properties of how the parts compose: latency adds along paths;. availability multiplies across hard dependencies;. failures travel along synchronous edges;.

54
00:04:22.368 --> 00:04:28.789
money is only safe if every step of a saga is idempotent and every abandoned saga is finished by someone.

55
00:04:29.069 --> 00:04:33.464
That is what an architect owns: not the boxes, but the arrows between them.

56
00:04:34.814 --> 00:04:35.497
Quote:

57
00:04:35.777 --> 00:04:37.441
The thesis of this chapter.

58
00:04:37.721 --> 00:04:44.526
A platform is correct only if its journeys are correct, fast and available end to end, not just its services.

59
00:04:44.806 --> 00:04:49.819
Budget latency along the critical path, and remove optional work from it (L1).

60
00:04:50.099 --> 00:04:59.657
Make every money-moving flow a saga with idempotent steps, explicit handling of unknown outcomes, and a sweeper that finishes abandoned work (L2).

61
00:04:59.937 --> 00:05:07.766
Draw the dependency graph and shrink the critical set: async side effects, local verification, degraded answers (L3).

62
00:05:09.116 --> 00:05:20.636
Set SLOs using composition arithmetic, engineer the small critical set to a higher standard than the journey, and use cells to bound how many customers one failure touches (L4).

