notifications, invoices and audit, per-service databases plus read models, and a lake for analytics.

757
00:45:21.888 --> 00:45:22.891
Question 2.

758
00:45:23.171 --> 00:45:25.496
What is a critical path, and why budget it?

759
00:45:25.776 --> 00:45:27.141
Thirty-second answer:

760
00:45:27.421 --> 00:45:41.800
The chain of calls a user action must complete; its latency is the sum of sequential steps and the max of parallel ones, and its tail compounds each hop's outliers, so each hop gets a budget and optional work leaves the path.

761
00:45:43.150 --> 00:45:44.195
Question 3.

762
00:45:44.475 --> 00:45:47.846
Why can't a checkout be more available than its dependencies?

763
00:45:48.126 --> 00:45:49.491
Thirty-second answer:

764
00:45:49.771 --> 00:46:05.835
If any hard dependency is down, the journey is down; with independent failures the journey's availability is at most the product of the dependencies' availabilities, 10 services at 99.95% give ~99.5%.

765
00:46:07.185 --> 00:46:08.252
Question 4.

766
00:46:08.532 --> 00:46:10.751
What does 'degraded mode' mean in practice?

767
00:46:11.031 --> 00:46:12.396
Thirty-second answer:

768
00:46:12.676 --> 00:46:27.865
A defined, honest reduced answer when an optional dependency fails or is slow: bestsellers instead of recommendations, category browse instead of search, no auto-coupon (with a message), cached serviceability, so the journey completes.

769
00:46:29.215 --> 00:46:30.218
Intermediate.

770
00:46:30.498 --> 00:46:31.607
Question 5.

771
00:46:31.887 --> 00:46:35.450
How do you make sure no customer is charged without getting an order?

772
00:46:35.730 --> 00:46:37.095
Thirty-second answer:

773
00:46:37.375 --> 00:46:56.490
Idempotent saga steps keyed by order id; treat payment timeouts as unknown and resolve via the gateway's status API; a saga log; a sweeper that completes or compensates abandoned sagas; customer notification on late completion; daily reconciliation with gateway settlement files.

774
00:46:57.840 --> 00:46:58.949
Question 6.

775
00:46:59.229 --> 00:47:03.432
How do you keep identity from being a platform-wide single point of failure?

776
00:47:03.712 --> 00:47:05.077
Thirty-second answer:

777
00:47:05.357 --> 00:47:17.197
Issue signed short-lived tokens and verify them locally at the gateway (public keys cached), so only login or refresh depends on identity; add revocation lists for emergencies.

778
00:47:18.547 --> 00:47:19.635
Question 7.

779
00:47:19.915 --> 00:47:21.793
Which work should leave the checkout path?

780
00:47:22.073 --> 00:47:23.438
Thirty-second answer:

781
00:47:23.718 --> 00:47:36.497
Anything the customer doesn't need to wait for: confirmation messages, invoices, audit records, fulfilment kickoff, analytics, loyalty, published as events via the outbox and consumed asynchronously.

782
00:47:37.847 --> 00:47:38.871
Question 8.

783
00:47:39.151 --> 00:47:41.135
What is a cell, and when do you use it?

784
00:47:41.415 --> 00:47:42.780
Thirty-second answer:

785
00:47:43.060 --> 00:47:57.609
A complete, independent copy of a stack serving a subset of customers (city group); failures and bad deploys stay inside one cell; use when blast radius matters and the domain partitions naturally (grocery by city).

786
00:47:58.959 --> 00:48:00.026
Question 9.

787
00:48:00.306 --> 00:48:01.949
How do you measure platform health?

788
00:48:02.229 --> 00:48:03.594
Thirty-second answer:

789
00:48:03.874 --> 00:48:18.167
Journey success rates and p99 per cell, saga health (stuck sagas, sweeper actions, reconciliation diffs), error budgets per journey, and business metrics (orders per minute, conversion), not just service dashboards.

790
00:48:19.517 --> 00:48:20.285
Senior.

791
00:48:20.565 --> 00:48:21.611
Question 10.

792
00:48:21.891 --> 00:48:25.347
How would you derive per-service SLOs from journey SLOs?

793
00:48:25.627 --> 00:48:26.992
Thirty-second answer:

794
00:48:27.272 --> 00:48:49.139
Start from the journey target (checkout 99.95%), list its hard dependencies (including infrastructure), allocate the unavailability budget across them (for example, 10 critical services at ~99.995%), reduce the list where the math doesn't work, and verify with measured journey availability.

795
00:48:50.489 --> 00:48:51.619
Question 11.

796
00:48:51.899 --> 00:48:54.331
How do you find hidden synchronous dependencies?

797
00:48:54.611 --> 00:48:55.977
Thirty-second answer:

798
00:48:56.257 --> 00:49:08.822
Build the dependency graph from distributed traces continuously, diff it against the intended architecture, flag new edges into the critical set, and enforce with CI fitness functions and ADRs.

799
00:49:10.172 --> 00:49:11.260
Question 12.

800
00:49:11.540 --> 00:49:13.204
How do you prepare for sale day?

801
00:49:13.484 --> 00:49:14.849
Thirty-second answer:

802
00:49:15.129 --> 00:49:29.807
Forecast load per journey; pre-scale; load-test journeys with failures; waiting room and admission control; freeze changes; verify degraded modes in game days; war room with journey dashboards; runbooks for top failure modes.

803
00:49:31.157 --> 00:49:32.437
Question 13.

804
00:49:32.717 --> 00:49:34.765
What order would you build this platform in?

805
00:49:35.045 --> 00:49:36.410
Thirty-second answer:

806
00:49:36.690 --> 00:49:54.205
Modular monolith with clean bounded contexts and one database first; extract checkout or payments, catalog or search and dispatch when load or team size demands; add the event backbone and read models; then cells and DR as scale and blast-radius needs grow.

807
00:49:55.555 --> 00:49:56.749
Question 14.

808
00:49:57.029 --> 00:49:59.525
How does money flow through the platform correctly?

809
00:49:59.805 --> 00:50:01.171
Thirty-second answer:

810
00:50:01.451 --> 00:50:18.688
Payment capture via gateway with idempotency; order snapshot with per-line allocations; double-entry ledger for wallets and refunds; settlements to sellers from the ledger; daily three-way reconciliation (orders, ledger, gateway); every step event-sourced or audited.

811
00:50:20.038 --> 00:50:20.934
Architect.

812
00:50:21.214 --> 00:50:22.409
Question 15.

813
00:50:22.689 --> 00:50:26.443
Design the entire quick-commerce platform in 45 minutes.

814
00:50:26.723 --> 00:50:28.089
Thirty-second answer:

815
00:50:28.369 --> 00:50:45.499
Clarify journeys and numbers; sketch layers; deep-dive checkout (saga, inventory, slots, payments) with failure handling; show dependency graph and degraded modes; cells and SLO arithmetic; data and events; security and compliance; cost and evolution.

816
00:50:46.849 --> 00:50:48.044
Question 16.

817
00:50:48.324 --> 00:50:51.759
How do you keep 26 services and 15 teams coherent?

818
00:50:52.039 --> 00:50:53.404
Thirty-second answer:

819
00:50:53.684 --> 00:51:13.183
Clear bounded contexts and ownership, ADRs and design reviews, platform paved roads (service template, observability, outbox, idempotency libraries), architecture fitness functions, journey SLOs owned jointly, and an architecture group that curates standards rather than approving every change.

820
00:51:14.533 --> 00:51:15.877
Question 17.

821
00:51:16.157 --> 00:51:20.039
What would you do in the first 90 days as architect of this platform?

822
00:51:20.319 --> 00:51:21.685
Thirty-second answer:

823
00:51:21.965 --> 00:51:38.263
Measure journeys end to end, map the real dependency graph from traces, audit money flows for unknown-outcome handling and sweepers, identify the critical set and its SLO math, fix the top blast-radius edges, and publish ADRs and a roadmap.

824
00:51:39.613 --> 00:51:40.787
Question 18.

825
00:51:41.067 --> 00:51:43.285
How do you handle a full region outage?

826
00:51:43.565 --> 00:51:44.931
Thirty-second answer:

827
00:51:45.211 --> 00:52:02.363
DR region with async replicas and tested runbooks; decide RPO or RTO per data class (payments near-zero RPO via synchronous replication or gateway reconciliation); stop the world, promote, reroute; reconcile after; practise it.

828
00:52:03.713 --> 00:52:04.950
Question 19.

829
00:52:05.230 --> 00:52:07.897
How do cost and reliability trade off here?

830
00:52:08.177 --> 00:52:09.542
Thirty-second answer:

831
00:52:09.822 --> 00:52:27.721
Reliability spend concentrates on the critical set (redundancy, cells, DR, higher engineering standards) while non-critical services run cheaper with degraded modes; measure cost per order and error budgets together; every extra nine should be justified by revenue at risk.

832
00:52:29.071 --> 00:52:30.180
Question 20.

833
00:52:30.460 --> 00:52:32.593
What makes this architecture 'done' for now?

834
00:52:32.873 --> 00:52:34.239
Thirty-second answer:

835
00:52:34.519 --> 00:52:51.799
Journey SLOs met with measured budgets, money invariants proven under failure, blast radius bounded by cells, dependency graph governed, costs per order known, and a roadmap for the next order of magnitude, reviewed continuously, because architectures are never finished.

